# Add a step to the development flow

A dfl flow is a state machine stored in `.df/<flow>/flow.json`. Every non-final state is a
**step** with a skill template at `.df/<flow>/steps/<step-id>/SKILL.md.hbs`; dfl renders it into
an Agent Skill (slash command) for every coding agent configured in this project. Your job is to
design the new step with the user, write its template and let dfl wire and install it.

{{#if flow.name}}
Active flow at the last sync: **{{flow.title}}** (`{{flow.name}}`), {{flow.stepCount}} steps:

{{#each flow.steps}}
{{number}}. **{{this.title}}** (`{{id}}`){{#each next}} [{{event}} → {{id}}]{{/each}}
{{else}}
(no steps yet)
{{/each}}

This is a snapshot; always read the live flow with `get_flow` before changing anything.
{{else}}
No flow was active at the last sync. Call `list_flows` and ask the user which flow to change
(every tool accepts a `flow` argument), or suggest `{{dfl.cli}} flow use <name>`.
{{/if}}

## Tools

Use the tools of the `{{dfl.mcpServer}}` MCP server (some agents list them with a prefix, such as
`mcp__{{dfl.mcpServer}}__add_step`). If they are not available, use the CLI fallbacks at the end.

## Procedure

1. **Read the flow.** Call `get_flow`: steps in order, their transitions (event → target), the
   initial step, the declared variables and which step is current.
2. **Read the authoring guide.** Call `get_authoring_guide` and follow it when writing the
   template. Call `get_step` on the existing step most similar to the new one and reuse its
   structure and tone, so the flow reads consistently.
3. **Interview the user.** Ask a few questions at a time and propose defaults they can accept:
   - **Purpose**: what the step achieves and when an agent should use it. Use the text the user
     typed after the command as the starting point.
   - **Id**: kebab-case (lowercase letters, digits, single hyphens), at most 64 characters,
     unique in the flow and different from the built-in skills `add-flow-step`,
     `edit-flow-step` and `delete-flow-step`. It becomes the skill name and slash command.
   - **Title**: one to three words, shown in lists and the flow viewer.
   - **Description**: one sentence of at most 200 characters that starts with a verb and says
     when to use the step. Agents use it to decide when to invoke the skill automatically.
   - **Position**: after which step (that step's `NEXT` then leads to the new step, and the new
     step's `NEXT` goes where the old one went) or before which step.
   - **Extra events**: for example `FAILED` back to an implementation step, `CHANGES_REQUESTED`,
     `BACK`. Events are UPPER_SNAKE_CASE; targets must be existing step ids or the final state.
   - **Inputs and outputs**: what the step reads (files from earlier steps, the text the user
     typed after the command) and what it produces (files, commits, reports); how "done" is
     decided.
   - **Variables**: team-wide values such as commands and paths are **project** variables
     (committed in `.df/config.json`); per-developer values such as handles or branch prefixes
     are **personal** variables (in the git-ignored `.df/local.json`, which is why rendered
     skills are git-ignored too). Reuse the variables from `get_flow` before inventing new ones.
4. **Draft the template body** following the guide:
   - No YAML frontmatter: dfl generates `name` and `description` from flow.json. Only
     `allowed-tools` or `license` may appear in frontmatter.
   - Sections: goal, inputs, a numbered procedure, outputs, exit criteria, pitfalls. Use concrete
     commands with variables, such as `\{{vars.testCommand}}`.
   - Context you can use: `\{{project.name}}`, `\{{flow.title}}`, `\{{step.title}}`,
     `\{{step.description}}`, `\{{vars.<name>}}`, `\{{env.<NAME>}}`, `\{{dfl.cli}}`; conditionals
     like `\{{#if vars.reviewer}}` ... `\{{/if}}`; loops like `\{{#each step.next}}` ... `\{{/each}}`;
     the helper `\{{command skillName}}` for another step's slash command.
   - The last line must be exactly `\{{> flow-context}}`. This partial appends the flow position,
     the next steps and the instructions for completing the step; do not write your own.
   - Never write the placeholders that agents expand when a skill runs as a command: a dollar
     sign followed by ARGUMENTS or by a digit, a line that begins with an exclamation mark
     followed by a backtick, or an at-sign directly followed by a word. Refer to arguments as
     "the text the user typed after the command".
   - Aim for 60 to 140 lines of instructions an agent can follow without guessing.
5. **Show the draft to the user**: id, title, description, position, the transitions before and
   after the change, new variables, and the full template. Iterate until they approve it.
6. **Create the step** with `add_step`: `id`, `title`, `description`, `after` or `before`,
   optionally `event` (the event used to splice the step in, default `NEXT`), `on` for extra
   transitions (for example `{ "FAILED": "implement-feature" }`) and `template`. dfl writes the
   files, re-renders and re-installs the skills, and returns the step, a sync summary and
   warnings.
7. **Declare new variables**, if any: `add_step` cannot do it. Add them to the `vars` object of
   `.df/<flow>/flow.json` (`description`, `default`, `scope`: `project` or `personal`,
   `required`), set values with `{{dfl.cli}} vars set <name> <value>` (add `--personal` for
   personal ones), then call `sync`.
8. **Validate** with `validate_flow`. Fix every error (usually with `update_step`) and explain the
   warnings to the user, for example a step that is unreachable from the initial step.
9. **Report**: the new slash command (from the `skillName` in the result), where the step sits
   (previous → new → next), and the files to commit (`git add .df/<flow>`) so the team gets the
   step; teammates run `{{dfl.cli}} flow-update` after pulling. Some agents only discover new
   skills after a restart or a skills reload; tell the user if the command does not show up.

Do not fire `transition`, `complete_step` or `goto_step` here: adding a step does not change
where the user currently is in the flow.

## CLI fallbacks

| MCP tool | CLI |
|----------|-----|
| `get_flow` | `{{dfl.cli}} step list` and read `.df/<flow>/flow.json` |
| `add_step` | `{{dfl.cli}} step add <id> --after <step> --title "<title>" --description "<description>"` (or `--before <step>`, `--event <EVENT>`) |
| `on` transitions | `{{dfl.cli}} step transition <id> <EVENT> <target>` |
| `template` | write `.df/<flow>/steps/<id>/SKILL.md.hbs`, then run `{{dfl.cli}} flow-update` |
| `validate_flow` | `{{dfl.cli}} flow validate` |
| `sync` | `{{dfl.cli}} flow-update` |

Without the MCP server there is no authoring guide tool; follow the rules in step 4.

## Pitfalls

- Edit only `.df/<flow>/...` files. Rendered `SKILL.md` files and the installed copies in agent
  skill folders are regenerated on every sync, so edits there are lost.
- Prefer the tools over hand-editing flow.json; if you do edit it, run `validate_flow` after.
- Keep one responsibility per step. If the user describes two, propose two steps.
